module Core

import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Base64 - the other notation a run of bytes ends up in, beside `Hex.yana` and on its shape.
   ==========================================================================================

   A digest, a key, a token and an embedded file are all a run of bytes that has to survive a text
   channel, and hexadecimal is the one this library had: two units a byte, which is a third longer
   than it needs to be and is what base64 exists to fix. So this is `Hex.yana` with a different
   alphabet - the same style-and-subject pair, the same `Flat(U8)` writing and `Array(U8)` reading,
   the same `readAll` for a whole string - and a reader of one has read the other.

   **Two alphabets and one implementation.** RFC 4648 §4 ends `+/` and §5 ends `-_`, and they differ
   in exactly those two units: everything else about the encoding is the same, so the alphabet is a
   pointer to one of two constants rather than two encoders. `url` picks §5 and turns padding off
   with it, because that pair is what a URL, a JWT and a file name all want and separating them would
   mean every call site set two flags that only ever move together - which `base64Url` then says in
   one word.

   **Three bytes at a time, and the tail written out.** The body of the loop is one twenty-four-bit
   word built from three loads and four table lookups, which is what makes this worth writing rather
   than expressing as a bit-at-a-time shift register. That loop is `pushBase64Groups`, the one body
   both targets share; natively `Base64.native.yana` replaces it with the block form `pushHex` has -
   twelve bytes spread into four lanes by one shuffle, the four windows cut out with shifts, and the
   alphabet one `lookup` of a sixteen-entry offset table (Analysis-Avx512.md §3.3).

   **The reader accepts either alphabet whichever the style names**, exactly as `hexValue` accepts
   either letter case: a reader that refused `-` because its style said `+` would be describing the
   text rather than reading it. `pad` is written and not read for the same reason - `=` is consumed
   where it appears and its absence ends the run, so a padded text and an unpadded one both read.
-}

{-
   RFC 4648 §4 and §5, whole rather than as a shared sixty-two units and a substitution: the
   substitution would be a branch inside the innermost loop to save sixty-two bytes of read-only
   data.

   A function answering a literal rather than two `let` globals and an `if` over them. A global is an
   owner, so choosing between two of them hands ownership out of storage the frame only borrows -
   which is what the ownership pass says, in those words. A literal in a result position is the
   constant itself, and the call folds to it wherever the flag is known, which it is at every call
   site this file has.
-}
fn base64Alphabet(url: Bool) -> String =
    if url then "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789-_"
    else "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789+/"

{-
   How to write a run of bytes as base64.

   `url` is the alphabet and `pad` is whether the tail is filled out to a multiple of four. They are
   separate fields because they are separate questions - a padded URL-safe text is legal and is what
   some libraries emit - and `base64Url` below sets both, because a call site that says "url" means
   both and should not have to remember the second.
-}
pub data Base64Style {
    url: Bool = False,
    pad: Bool = True
}

-- The subject is `[U8]` for `HexFormat`'s reason: `Formatted`'s `value: 'a` is a declared borrow, and
-- a stored borrow of a container is that container's window - so one head serves both spellings.
pub alias Base64Format = Formatted(Base64Style, [U8])

pub fn base64Style(url: Bool = False, pad: Bool = True) = Base64Style {url: url, pad: pad}
pub fn base64UrlStyle() = Base64Style {url: True, pad: False}

pub fn base64(bytes: '[U8], url: Bool = False, pad: Bool = True) -> Base64Format =
    Formatted {value: bytes, style: base64Style(url, pad)}

-- The §5 form under its own name, because a call site putting bytes in a URL should say that rather
-- than set two flags a reader has to recombine.
pub fn base64Url(bytes: '[U8]) -> Base64Format =
    Formatted {value: bytes, style: base64UrlStyle()}

{-
   The whole-string form, which is `hexOf`'s counterpart and exists for its reason: a program that
   wants the text as a *value* rather than as part of a bigger one should not have to make a sink to
   get it. The sink is sized exactly - four units per three bytes, rounded up - so this is one
   allocation and no growth.
-}
pub fn base64Of(bytes: [U8], url: Bool = False, pad: Bool = True) -> String:
    let &text = String.ofCapacity(((length(bytes) + 2) / 3) * 4)
    showWith(Formatted {value: bytes, style: base64Style(url, pad)} :: Base64Format, text)
    return text

{-
   The whole three-byte groups, written as four units each, and where they stopped - every byte but
   the last one or two, which `showWith` pads.

   One twenty-four-bit word, four six-bit windows out of it, and no test inside the body: the tail is
   what the loop stops in front of. This is the baseline, and the meaning; `Base64.native.yana` replaces
   it with a body that does twelve bytes a turn.
-}
fn pushBase64Groups(bytes: [U8], url: Bool, &to: String) -> Size:
    let alphabet = base64Alphabet(url)
    let count = length(bytes)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i + 3 <= count:
        let word = ((bytes[i] :: Int) `shl` 16) `or` ((bytes[i + 1] :: Int) `shl` 8) `or` (bytes[i + 2] :: Int)

        String.pushUnit(to, String.unit(alphabet, ((word `shr` 18) `and` 63) :: Size))
        String.pushUnit(to, String.unit(alphabet, ((word `shr` 12) `and` 63) :: Size))
        String.pushUnit(to, String.unit(alphabet, ((word `shr` 6) `and` 63) :: Size))
        String.pushUnit(to, String.unit(alphabet, (word `and` 63) :: Size))

        i = i + 3

    return i

instance ShowWith(Base64Style, [U8]):
    fn showWith(subject: Base64Format, &to: String):
        let alphabet = base64Alphabet(subject.style.url)
        let bytes = subject.value
        let count = length(bytes)

        let i = pushBase64Groups(bytes, subject.style.url, to)

        let left = count - i
        if left == 0 then return

        -- One or two bytes, which become two or three units and are padded out to four. The word is
        -- built the same way and the missing bytes are zeros, which is what makes the last window's
        -- low bits zero rather than something a reader would have to be told to ignore.
        let word = ((bytes[i] :: Int) `shl` 16) `or` (if left == 2 then (bytes[i + 1] :: Int) `shl` 8 else 0)

        String.pushUnit(to, String.unit(alphabet, ((word `shr` 18) `and` 63) :: Size))
        String.pushUnit(to, String.unit(alphabet, ((word `shr` 12) `and` 63) :: Size))

        if left == 2 then String.pushUnit(to, String.unit(alphabet, ((word `shr` 6) `and` 63) :: Size))
        else if subject.style.pad then String.pushUnit(to, "=")

        if subject.style.pad then String.pushUnit(to, "=")

    -- Exact for a padded run and generous by one or two units for an unpadded one, which is the
    -- direction a bound is allowed to be wrong in. A runtime value, like `Show(String)`'s.
    fn showBoundWith(subject: Base64Format) -> Maybe(Size) =
        Just(((length(subject.value) + 2) / 3) * 4)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Reading a run back
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The dual, and the same style. **A group is four units and a shorter one is a shorter run**: three
   units make two bytes and two make one, which is what an unpadded text ends in, and a *single*
   trailing unit encodes no whole byte at all - so the run stops in front of it and the cursor is
   left there. That is `readHexBytes`' answer to an odd trailing digit, made at the group size this
   notation has.

   `=` is consumed where it appears and ends the run, so a padded text reads to the same bytes an
   unpadded one does. A text of no groups at all is `NoDigits`, on `Core/Read.yana`'s terms: nothing
   was consumed and the cursor did not move.
-}
{-
   One group's six-bit values, packed most significant first, with `held` saying how many arrived.

   A function rather than the loop written inside the reader because a group is a thing with a name
   and two answers, which is what a function is for - and no longer because of the JS backend, which
   until 2026-08-30 refused `while a && b:` with a `break` in it outright and made this loop's bound
   a value computed in front of it. See `test/resolve/LoopBreak.yana` for what that was.
-}
fn base64Group(text: String, from: Size, &held: Int) -> Int:
    let units = length(text)

    let &word = 0
    let &at = from

    while at < from + 4 && at < units:
        let value = base64Value(String.unit(text, at))
        if value < 0 then break

        word = (word `shl` 6) `or` value
        at = at + 1

    held = truncate(at - from) :: Int
    return word

-- And the `=` that fills a short group out, which is consumed with it. At most as many as the group
-- is missing, so a run of them past the end of one is the caller's text rather than this one's.
fn base64Padding(text: String, from: Size, held: Int) -> Size:
    let units = length(text)

    let &at = from
    let &filled = held

    while filled < 4 && at < units && String.unit(text, at) == "=":
        at = at + 1
        filled = filled + 1

    return at

{-
   The whole four-unit groups at the head of a run, decoded a block at a time - the dual of
   `pushBase64Groups`, split the same way and for the same reason.

   What it promises is narrow on purpose: **a position at or after `from`, with exactly the bytes of
   the groups between the two appended**. Every unit it consumes is a unit of one of the alphabets
   and every group it consumes is a whole four, so what is left for the loop below is a run that
   starts at a group boundary - which is the state that loop already begins in.

   **The baseline decodes nothing, and that is a body rather than a stub.** "No groups" satisfies the
   promise above, the loop below finishes the run whichever body ran, and the bytes and the cursor
   are therefore the same either way - which is the property the suite's `no-replacements` row
   checks. A JavaScript build has nothing to choose, as it has nothing to choose for `itemsEqual`,
   and for the same reason: a host vector is `lanes` values and the block form would be the walk with
   an array allocated per iteration.
-}
fn pushBase64Blocks(text: String, from: Size, &bytes: Array(U8)) -> Size = from

instance ReadWith(Base64Style, [U8]):
  fn readWith(style: Base64Style, from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, [U8]):
    let units = length(from)
    let &->bytes = emptyArray() :: [U8]

    -- Three bytes per four units, which is exact for a padded text and one or two long for the
    -- others - the same direction of generosity the writer's bound has.
    reserve(bytes, ((units - at) / 4) * 3)

    let &start = at
    let &any = False

    -- The blocks first, where there is a body for them: what comes back is a group boundary, and
    -- what is left is whatever the block form would not take - a short group, a padded one, or the
    -- first unit that is not base64 at all.
    let blocked = pushBase64Blocks(from, start, bytes)
    if blocked > start:
        any = True
        start = blocked

    while start < units:
        let &held = 0 :: Int
        let packed = base64Group(from, start, held)

        -- A lone unit is six bits of nothing, so the group is not taken and neither is it: the
        -- cursor stops in front of it, which is what lets a caller see where the run really ended.
        if held < 2 then break

        -- The bits the group did not carry, shifted out from the bottom so the bytes below are the
        -- ones the writer put in.
        let word = packed `shl` (6 * (4 - held))

        push(bytes, truncate((word `shr` 16) `and` 255) :: U8)
        if held >= 3 then push(bytes, truncate((word `shr` 8) `and` 255) :: U8)
        if held == 4 then push(bytes, truncate(word `and` 255) :: U8)

        any = True
        start = start + (held :: Size)

        -- A group that did not fill is the last one, whether it was padded or simply ran out - a
        -- short group is only produced at the end of a text, so what follows it is the caller's.
        -- The padding that fills it out is this text's own and is consumed with it, which is what
        -- makes `Zg==rest` read one byte and leave the cursor on the `r`.
        if held < 4:
            start = base64Padding(from, start, held)
            break

    if !any then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = start
    return Ok(bytes)

pub fn readBase64(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, [U8]) =
    readWith(base64Style(), from, at)

{-
   One unit's six-bit value, or `-1` for a unit that is not one.

   **Both alphabets at once**, which is the reader's whole tolerance: `+` and `-` are 62 and `/` and
   `_` are 63, so a text written under either style reads under either. `digitValue` in
   `Core/Read.yana` is the same shape for the same reason, and the ranges are in the order a base64
   text's units actually fall in - letters first, then digits, then the two that are neither.

   A sentinel rather than a `Maybe`, unlike `hexValue` beside it: that one is a `@bits(4) U32` whose
   niche makes the wrapper free, and six bits have no spare values in a byte to donate. So this is
   the one place in the directory where `-1` is still the answer, and the reason is the layout rather
   than a preference.
-}
pub fn base64Value(unit: Int) -> Int:
    if unit >= "A" && unit <= "Z" then return unit - "A"
    if unit >= "a" && unit <= "z" then return unit - "a" + 26
    if unit >= "0" && unit <= "9" then return unit - "0" + 52
    if unit == "+" || unit == "-" then return 62
    if unit == "/" || unit == "_" then return 63
    return -1
